This means if the `order_by_param` doesn't match any of the hardcoded allowlisted values (like "priority", "-priority", "state__group", "-state__group", etc.), it will be passed directly to Django's `.order_by()` method without any validation. While Django's ORM `.order_by()` is generally safer than raw SQL, it can still be vulnerable if an attacker passes arbitrary field names or SQL expressions that aren't properly validated.

The issue is that the user input flows from line 1060 into `order_issue_queryset()` and if it doesn't match the explicit conditions (lines 22-68), it reaches line 82 where it's used in `.order_by(order_by_param)` without validation.

